+# Quadratic quartet backreaction: personal N1-N8 review
+
+No broad negative claim is made. The linear conserved mean and pure power
+law are corrected within a specified second-order one-loop expansion.
+
+## N1 — Routes actually checked
+
+| Family | Work | Disposition |
+|---|---|---|
+| Feynman parameter integral | Expand the full four-dimensional integrand through second order | ATTEMPTED; explicit quadratic tensor coefficient |
+| Unshifted loop integral | Exact S3 second, fourth and sixth moments | ATTEMPTED; separate algebraic recovery of that coefficient |
+| Scalar velocity formula | Expand the exact fixed-photon scalar expression | ATTEMPTED; checks normalization and quadratic sign |
+| Positive photon tensors | Extract the metric from epsilon-b and its trace | ATTEMPTED; quadratic photon-metric source |
+| Coupled mode equations | Exact integration, including resonance and source cancellation | ATTEMPTED; logarithmic quartet correction and finite common drift |
+| Constitutive history | Integrate full positive tensors along the truncated trajectories | ATTEMPTED; challenges drift and unchanged quadratic polarization |
+
+These are checks of one formal calculation, not independent phase routes.
+
+## N2 — Dependencies
+
+The real coframe drift, mean resonance and polarization use one supplied
+massless Hall-free loop action. They do not count as separate axiom walls.
+The direct history calculation checks the same coefficient expansion.
+
+## N3 — Premises
+
+Unit charges, the correct Weyl bubble count, a gauge-preserving weak-coupling
+prescription, nonmerging nodes, untilted spatial coframes and trace-free
+first-order contrasts are explicit. First-order mean data vanish in this
+subproblem. Second-order initial mean data remain allowed. Initial photon
+birefringence is at most second order. A common coordinate choice does not
+set a physical speed. The initial coframe square variance is unnormalized
+by its trace and the full relative correction includes an isotropic part.
+
+## N4 — Source match
+
+The full parameter integral and scalar comparator are directly supplied in
+the current Hall/cone source. Block11 supplies the actual quartet contrast
+and node count. Its quadratic polarization depends only on first-order
+metrics, so this second-order common drift does not alter that coefficient.
+No short-range Weyl theorem or all-order QED construction is imported.
+
+## N5 — Resolution
+
+Per tensor component: exact eigenvalue polynomial and independent angular
+moments. Per normalization: temporal wavefunction subtraction and scalar
+formula. Per mode: all matrices in the mean/contrast sector of the stated
+expansion; N2 resonance and N3 cancellation retained. Per trajectory:
+positive finite constitutive tensors. Lattice-wide: no interacting
+thermodynamic phase or uniform remainder estimate is computed.
+
+## N6 — Partial closure
+
+The corrected truncated flow still approaches a common metric. The mean
+correction is log(z)/z², while the contrasts and polarization have slower
+1/z contributions. The common limiting value depends on input contrasts.
+A genuine full-flow theorem would need bounds on omitted terms, plus the
+actual phase. Nothing here rules out either task or requires a new axiom.
+
+## N7 — Steelman
+
+A critic can correctly reject an all-orders attraction claim: finitely many
+beta coefficients and an exact solution of their truncation do not control
+the full physical flow. The note makes no such claim. Its deliverable is a
+specific nonzero quadratic term that a stronger theory must retain.
+
+## N8 — Prior comparison
+
+The current parent gave the linear coframe flow and a quadratic nonmetric
+photon source. Block11 added quartet contrasts. The present delta is the
+quadratic matter coframe coefficient, metric photon increment and the
+actual quartet's resonant relative mean. General perturbative velocity
+renormalization is established prior physics. No broad novelty is claimed.
